tions**: Once the entities are set up, you&#x27;ll use the intercompany accounts to record transactions between them. This does require manual entry and consistent tracking.\\n\\n### Comparison with Excel:\\n- **Excel**: More customizable but completely manual. You create your spreadsheet and manually enter all transactions.\\n- **QBO**: Integrated with your overall accounting but still requires manual entry for intercompany transactions. It has the benefit of being within your existing accounting system, allowing for easier reconciliation and reporting.\\n\\n### My Opinion:\\n- **Pros of QBO**: Integration with your existing accounting, easier reconciliation, and potential automation with banking feeds.\\n- **Cons of QBO**: Might require setting up new companies within QBO and some manual tracking.\\n\\nIf your companies are already distinct within QBO, you won&#x27;t need to set up new companies. If not, creating separate companies is typically recommended for clarity and compliance. \\n\\nI hope this helps clarify the process! Feel free to reach out if you have further questions or need additional assistance."\highlight29\cf28\b ]\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "status"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "finished_successfully"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "end_turn"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 true\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "weight"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight21\cf20 1.0\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "metadata"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "finish_details"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "type"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "stop"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "stop_tokens"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17