;body&gt;` section, you can add form elements such as labels, input fields, checkboxes, and dropdowns. Use HTML tags like `&lt;form&gt;`, `&lt;label&gt;`, `&lt;input&gt;`, `&lt;select&gt;`, and `&lt;textarea&gt;`. For dynamic data, include placeholders that will be replaced with values from Dynamics 365. Here&#x27;s an example:\\n```\\n&lt;body&gt;\\n&lt;form&gt;\\n    &lt;div class=&quot;form-field&quot;&gt;\\n        &lt;label for=&quot;name&quot; class=&quot;label&quot;&gt;Name:&lt;/label&gt;\\n        &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; id=&quot;name&quot; name=&quot;name&quot; placeholder=&quot;[Name Placeholder]&quot;&gt;\\n    &lt;/div&gt;\\n    &lt;div class=&quot;form-field&quot;&gt;\\n        &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot; class=&quot;label&quot;&gt;Email:&lt;/label&gt;\\n        &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; placeholder=&quot;[Email Placeholder]&quot;&gt;\\n    &lt;/div&gt;\\n    &lt;!-- Add more form elements and placeholders --&gt;\\n&lt;/form&gt;\\n&lt;/body&gt;\\n```\\n\\n5. Save the HTML template: Save the file with a .html extension, for example, &quot;state_form_template.html&quot;. Choose a location where you can easily access it.\\n\\nThat&#x27;s it! You now have a basic HTML template for your state form. You can customize and expand it according to your specific requirements, adding more form fields, formatting, and styling as needed.\\n\\nRemember to replace the placeholders like `[Name Placeholder]` and `[Email Placeholder]` with the actual field names or data placeholders from Dynamics 365 when you populate the template in Power Automate.\\n\\n"\highlight29\cf28\b ]\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "status"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "finished_successfully"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "end_turn"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 true\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "w