iana. The current corporate income tax rate in Indiana is a flat rate of 5.5%.\\n   - Franchise Taxes: Indiana also imposes an annual franchise tax on LLCs, which is based on the LLC&#x27;s income or the value of its property located in Indiana, whichever is greater. The franchise tax rate is currently $1.50 per $1,000 of the LLC&#x27;s adjusted gross income or property value.\\n   - Federal Taxes: The LLC would also be subject to federal income taxes. By default, LLCs are treated as pass-through entities for tax purposes, meaning the income and expenses &quot;pass through&quot; to the owners&#x27; individual tax returns. The LLC itself does not pay federal income tax, but the owners report their share of the LLC&#x27;s income on their personal tax returns and pay taxes accordingly.\\n\\n2. Sole Proprietorship Tax Liabilities:\\n   - State Taxes: As a sole proprietor operating in Indiana, the state tax obligations would be similar to those of an LLC. You would need to pay Indiana state income tax on the net income derived from your business activities in Indiana. The current individual income tax rates in Indiana range from 3.23% to 5.75%, depending on income levels.\\n   - Federal Taxes: Similar to an LLC, as a sole proprietor, you would report your business income and expenses on your personal tax return. The net income from your sole proprietorship is subject to federal income tax at your individual tax rate.\\n\\n3. Florida Tax Considerations:\\n   - Since your LLC is based in Florida, it&#x27;s important to note that Florida does not have a state income tax on individuals or businesses. However, if the LLC generates income from Indiana sources, you would still have tax obligations in Indiana as discussed above.\\n   - As a sole proprietor, if you live in Florida but your business operates in Indiana, you would generally still need to pay Indiana state income tax on the income derived from your Indiana business activities.\\n\\nIt&#x27;s important to consider other tax obligations such as sales tax, employment taxes, and any additional local or industry-specific taxes that may apply to your specific business activities. Consulting with a tax professional will help ensure compliance with all applicable tax laws and maximize potential tax benefits."\highlight29\cf28\b ]\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "status"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "finished_successfully"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "end_turn"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 true\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "weight"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight21\cf20 1.0\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "metadata"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "finish_details"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "type"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "stop"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "stop"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "&lt;|diff_marker|&gt;"\highlight29\cf28\b \},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "is_complete"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 true\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "message_type"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 null\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "model_slug"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "text-davinci-002-render-sha"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "timestamp_"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "absolute"\highlight29\cf28\b \},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "recipient"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "all"\highlight29\cf28\b \},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "parent"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "aaa212b6-72d9-44cf-bed7-b2e76fdd8fca"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "children"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b [\highlight27\cf26\b0 "aaa287b3-3f9d-4aa0-bbf8-013b71044779"\highlight29\cf28\b ]\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "aaa287b3-3f9d-4aa0-bbf8-013b71044779"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "id"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "aaa287b3-3f9d-4aa0-bbf8-013b71044779"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "message"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "id"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "aaa287b3-3f9d-4aa0-bbf8-013b71044779"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "author"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "role"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "user"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "name"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 null\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "metadata"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\}\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "create_time"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight21\cf20 1688984258.042859\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "update_time"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 null\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "content"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b \{\highlight27\cf26\b0 "content_type"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "text"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "parts"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight29\cf28\b [\highlight27\cf26\b0 "Using the following information can you write a summary of a recommendation to this client. The client is CANVAS PAD and I am their Accountant my name is Cody Rice-Velasquez of Servicios Rice-Velasquez, Their quesitons was: They are planning to move to Florida but keep the business in Indiana and they wanted to know what the tax implications were and if their legal address should be for the residence in florida or residence in indiana. Here are some research details to help you formulate a good summary for the client. SUMMARY FROM BING:\\n## Bing\\nIf you are planning to claim residency in another state other than Indiana to avoid tax penalties, you should be aware that dual state residency can result in dual taxation. This means that you may be taxed by both states. \\nTo avoid or at least minimize failure to pay penalties, pay your tax in full by the tax deadline, even if you request an extension. If you owe more than you can afford to pay, pay as much as possible by the deadline, then pay the rest as soon as you can. \\nHere are four steps you can take to help avoid complications if you live and work in two different states:\\n - Let your employer\\u2019s HR department know which state you live in and ask about the rules. If the two states have reciprocity, your employer should be able to withhold taxes from your pay for your state of residence.\\n - File a tax return in each state where you worked and earned income.\\n - Claim a credit for taxes paid to another state on your resident state tax return.\\n - Keep good records.\\n\\nI hope this helps!\\n\\nSUMMARY FROM STACKEXCHANGE\\nMany well-off people seek to establish residency in Florida to take advantage of the fact that the state has no income taxes. In order to establish Florida residency, you must be physically present in Florida for 183 days of the tax year (with parts of a day counting as a full day). It also helps to establish a domicile, with a driver&#x27;s license, vehicle registration, and home ownership located in the state.\\n8\\n\\nAll states have figured out that this is a &#x27;great&#x27; and seemingly &#x27;easy&#x27; way to dodge taxes, so in my experience if you work/live in multiple states it doesn&#x27;t matter how honest or correct your taxes are - they will all often request more paperwork asking you to prove what you say about your residency is true. If you do not provide it, they will assume the truth is whatever gets them the most money, and the onus becomes on you to prove otherwise. And the documents they require quickly become non-trivial.\\n\\nAt one point I had to provide copies of birth certificates, driver license and registration, car insurance, lease(s), school records, a signed/notarized statement from myself asserting my proper status, W2s from every job, copies of tax returns in every state I filed and a copy of my federal return, etc. And I had to do something similar for every state I filed in. It all worked out fine - but every state was very keen to cut down on this form of tax fraud (this happened within the last few years).\\n\\nStates with low/no income taxes don&#x27;t like being used for this, because they lose money too - those states generally rely on other taxes which you will contribute minimally to. States with income taxes where you work and claim not to live aren&#x27;t keen on such a situation either, because they lose a primary source of income.\\n\\nIndiana Tax Penalties and Law\\nTax Penalties\\nFailure to pay tax: 10% of the unpaid tax liability or $5, whichever is greater. This penalty is also imposed on payments which are required to be remitted electronically, but are not.\\nFailure to file a tax return; preparation by Department: 20% penalty.\\nFailure to file a tax return; fraudulent intent to evade tax: 100% penalty\\nFaulty payment (e.g. bad checks): A flat $35 fee will be charged.\\nFailure to file information return: a penalty of $10 for each failure to file a timely return, not to exceed $25,000 in any one calendar year, is imposed. This penalty is also imposed if the information return is required to be filed electronically, but is not.\\nFailure to file corporate or partnership tax return reporting zero tax liability: The penalty for failure to file by the due date is $10 per day that the return is past due, up to $250. In addition, a separate $10 penalty is assessed on each Schedule K1 information return that is late.\\nMany people feel that taxes are too high, but most don&#x27;t go so far as to not pay when the tax bill comes around. What happens if you don&#x27;t pay your taxes in Indiana? Tax fraud or tax evasion is when someone intentionally doesn&#x27;t pay or underpays his or her taxes. This can happen by purposefully completing forms inaccurately in order to owe less to the state.\\n\\nTax fraud or evasion is illegal in Indiana and you could be subject to both criminal and administrative penalties, depending on the fraud perpetrated and your intent. If you or a loved one are facing a tax related charge, you should contact a qualified local criminal defense attorney who specializes in tax fraud cases for assistance.\\n\\nTax Fraud and Tax Evasion Penalties\\nTax fraud and tax evasion are white collar crimes. Tax evaders typically owe 100% of the correctly calculated unpaid taxes as well as the cost to the state of Indiana for the extra effort to collect the taxes. The taxpayer, whether an individual, partnership, or corporation, can be found have evaded their taxes and both can be penalized accordingly.\\n\\nPenalties can be even more severe for tax professionals, such as CPAs, who perpetrate tax fraud. For example, when CPAs fail to file or fraudulent file their own income tax returns, they can be sanctioned, possibly losing their license to practice and thus their employment.\\n\\nIndiana Tax Fraud and Tax Evasion Laws: Statutes\\nDetails on Indiana tax fraud and tax evasion laws are outline below:\\n\\nCode Sections\\n\\nIndiana Code Title 6: Taxation contains the tax laws for income, property, sales, cigarette, and use taxes, as well as the penalties for fraud and evasion of these taxes.\\n\\nConsumers may be most interested in the income tax fraud sections at: 6-3-6-11: Evasion of Tax and 6-3-6-10: Taxpayer&#x27;s Record Keeping Requirements &amp; False Entries\\n\\nWhat&#x27;s Prohibited?\\n\\nIt&#x27;s illegal for a taxpayer to fail to file a state income tax return (when required), make a false return with intent to defraud state, evade paying the tax, not allow the Indiana Department of Revenue to examine your records, or refuse to produce testimony or records as required by law. Any of these things are a Level 6 felony. Both the attorney general and prosecuting attorney can enforce this law.\\n\\nTaxpayers must keep and preserve their records (as required) so they can be examined by the Department of Revenue, as needed. Failure to do so is a Class A misdemeanor. However, if a taxpayer makes false record entries AKA &quot;cooks the books&quot; with the intent to defraud the state or avoid paying taxes, it&#x27;s a Level 6 felony.\\n\\nSimilar tax evasion laws exist for other, non-income, taxes in Indiana, including utility receipt, cigarette, and petroleum severance taxes.\\n\\nStatute of Limitations\\n\\nGenerally, crimes must be prosecuted within a specific period of time, called the statute of limitations. Typically, Indiana has a three-year statute of limitations for tax liabilities. However, when a return is not filed, is blank or unsigned, or is fraudulent, the three-year clock doesn&#x27;t start running. This permits even old tax fraud to be pursued. Fraudulent returns require:\\n\\nMisrepresentation of a fact\\nGuilty knowledge by taxpayer of the falsity of the fact\\nDeceiving the Department of Revenue (DOR)\\nThe DOR relies on the fraud\\nThe DOR is injured, usually shown by not being able to collect the tax owed\\nPenalty Jail and Fine Schedule\\n\\nPunishments for felonies and misdemeanors in Indiana vary by level or class. The penalty ranges for the tax fraud and tax evasion crimes listed above are:\\n\\nLevel 6 Felony - 6 months to 2.5 years in county jail and a fine and a fine up to $10,000\\nClass A Misdemeanor - up to 1 year in county jail and a fine of up to $5,000\\nAdditionally, a fee to recover the expenses incurred by the state of Indiana for investigating and prosecuting the tax fraud or evasion can be levied on the evading taxpayer.\\n\\nNote: Indiana criminal law transitioned from classifying felonies as Classes A-D to Levels 1-6 on July 1, 2014. Some individuals, legal self-help literature, or websites may still refer to the old system.\\n\\nNote: State laws are constantly changing -- it&#x27;s important to verify the state law(s) you are researchi\\n\\nMY SUMMARY: \\nWHILE THE PROSPECT OF USING YOUR OTHER HOME IN FLORIDA AS YOUR MAIN LEGAL ADDRESS FOR TAX PURPOSES WOULD PROVIDE A TAX BENEFIT FOR STATE TAXES AS FLORIDA DOES NOT REQUIRE THEM AND YOU WOULD NOT BE CHARGED STATE INCOME TAX ON YOUR LLC INCOME (BASED ON THE INFORMATION CURRENTLY AVAILABLE AT THE TIME OF THE REVIEW) I WOULD STILL SUGGEST UNLESS YOUR ARE SPENDING 6 MONTHS OR MORE (183 DAYS ACCORDING TO MOST STATES) IN FLORIDA I CANNOT RECOMMEND DOING THIS. IF YOU ARE NOT ABLE TO PROVE THAT YOU ARE A BONIFIED RESIDENT OF FLORIDA BASED ON THE STATUES YOU COULD RISK BEING CONVICTED OF TAX FRAUD AND JAILED OR AT THE VERY LIST PAY LARGE TAX PENALTIES. "\highlight29\cf28\b ]\},\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "status"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "finished_successfully"\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "end_turn"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight25\cf24\b\i0 null\highlight29\cf28\i0 ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "weight"\highlight29\cf28\b :\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight21\cf20 1.0\highlight29\cf28\b ,\highlight17\cf16\b0  \highlight27\cf26 "metadata"\highlig